==================================================================
OBSERVATIONS (not criterion failures; for the lead)
==================================================================
O1. The Go glue matches on the check NAME only (`RESULT guard_directions`),
    not on `NAME PASS`. I probed it: a harness printing all three names
    marked FAIL and exiting 0 passes the Go test (.../a2/vac6, vac6_EXIT=0).
    This is NOT reachable in the shipped harness -- main() derives the
    printed verdict and the exit code from the same `allPass` variable
    (harness :336-340), so they cannot diverge -- and every attack the brief
    names is blocked. But `regexp.QuoteMeta(name + " PASS")` would have cost
    nothing and closed it. Belt-and-braces gap, recorded honestly.
O2. The harness's catch block labels a thrown sequence with the JS function
    name (`testDismissResolveRecreate`) rather than the snake_case check name
    (`dismiss_resolve_recreate`), so a crash reports under a name the Go glue
    is not looking for. Consequence is a stricter failure, never a weaker
    one (V4 above). Cosmetic.
O3. `make race`, `test-unit`, `test-integration` and `test-coverage` do not
    depend on check-node, so on a node-less machine they would skip the
    harness silently. `make test`, `make check` and `make check-full` all
    enforce it. Worth a one-line fix if the lead wants uniformity.
O4. The harness stubs the DOM rather than rendering the real Go template, so
    it validates the <script>'s BEHAVIOUR but not that the server-rendered
    region text and data-warnings-text agree. That agreement is what attempt
    1's whitespace-padding finding was about, and it is covered by the Go
    handler tests. Noted so the division of labour is on the record, not as
    a gap.

==================================================================
BOTTOM LINE
==================================================================
The one thing attempt 1 lacked now exists and demonstrably bites. The exact
command that exposed the attempt-1 failure -- restoring the authenticated
pre-S5 accounts.html and running `go test ./...` -- went from exit 0 to exit
1. The check reads the live template at run time (proven by mutating the
template under an already-compiled test binary), discriminates a real
structural divergence from a pure copy edit in both directions, cannot be
satisfied vacuously by six different attacks, skips with a named reason
where node is absent, and is hard-required by `make test` through a
prerequisite chain confirmed against make's own database. Attempt 1's five
files are byte-identical. No new dependency, no package.json, no critical
glob touched. PASS.
